   "evidence": "`grep '\u2016fourier' mathlib/Mathlib` yields no global pointwise-norm lemma; Mathlib has it only as a local `have` in `fourier_norm` (mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/Fourier/AddCircle.lean:183: `have : \u2200 x : AddCircle T, \u2016fourier n x\u2016 = 1 := fun x => Circle.norm_coe _`). `fourier_apply` is `@[simp]` (mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/Fourier/AddCircle.lean:127-129) and `Circle.norm_coe` is `@[simp]` (mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/Complex/Circle.lean:74); Mathlib deliberately normalises away from `fourier` toward `toCircle` (see the `-- simp normal form is `fourier_zero'`` comment at AddCircle.lean:145).",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Mathematics/Analysis/Fourier/AddCircle.lean",
   "fix": "Add `@[simp] theorem norm_fourier_apply (n : \u2124) (x : AddCircle T) : \u2016fourier n x\u2016 = 1 := Circle.norm_coe _` to this file and use it explicitly at the seven sites, so the bound rests on a named fact rather than on `fourier`'s `toCircle` simp normal form.",
   "issue": "The reusable bound `\u2016fourier n x\u2016 = 1` \u2014 the fact the new file's own docstring cites (\"being bounded by `1`\") \u2014 is never stated; it is re-proved by a bare `simp` that works only because `@[simp] fourier_apply` unfolds `fourier n x` to `\u2191(toCircle (n \u2022 x))` and `@[simp] Circle.norm_coe` then applies. Seven dependent sites: this file lines 45 and 55 (the `.of_bound`/`.mul_bdd` bounds), and `Nuclear/Medium/Collective/Basic.lean` lines 116 (`norm_qVector_add_const`), 126 (`norm_qVector_le_card`), 135 (`ofReal_norm_qVector_sq`, diagonal term via `Complex.mul_conj'`), 235 (`norm_anisotropyCoeff_le_one`) and 260 (`norm_anisotropyCoeff_map_add_const`).",
   "line": "39"
  }
 ],
